-- Q7 [Nernst Equation & Concentration Cells · hard · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans C] (g75)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Nernst Equation & Concentration Cells',
    $qt75$A galvanic cell is constructed according to the notation \(\text{Pb(s)}\,|\,\text{Pb}^{2+}(0.10\ \text{M})\,||\,\text{Ag}^{+}(0.0010\ \text{M})\,|\,\text{Ag(s)}\), which operates by the overall reaction \(\text{Pb(s)} + 2\,\text{Ag}^{+} \rightarrow \text{Pb}^{2+} + 2\,\text{Ag(s)}\). The standard cell potential is \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = +0.93\ \text{V}\) and the cell runs at \(298\ \text{K}\), where \(\frac{0.0592}{n}\log Q\) gives the Nernst correction. Which value most nearly equals the actual cell potential \(E_{\text{cell}}\) under these non-standard concentrations?$qt75$,
    $op75$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(+1.08\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(+0.90\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(+0.78\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(+1.22\\ \\text{V}\\)"}]$op75$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex75$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because the Nernst equation at 298 K is \(E_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} - \frac{0.0592}{n}\log Q\). First build the reaction quotient, excluding the pure solids Pb(s) and Ag(s): \(Q = \frac{[\text{Pb}^{2+}]}{[\text{Ag}^{+}]^{2}}\). Because Ag\(^{+}\) carries a coefficient of 2, its concentration is squared: \(Q = \frac{0.10}{(0.0010)^{2}} = \frac{0.10}{1.0\times10^{-6}} = 1.0\times10^{5}\), so \(\log Q = 5\) (not 1). With \(n = 2\) electrons transferred, \(\frac{0.0592}{n} = \frac{0.0592}{2} = 0.0296\). Then \(E_{\text{cell}} = 0.93 - (0.0296)(5) = 0.93 - 0.148 = 0.782\ \text{V} \approx +0.78\ \text{V}\). The potential is lowered (but stays positive, so the cell is still spontaneous) because \(Q > 1\): the products are favored relative to standard conditions, which by Le Chatelier reduces the driving force. (Choice A) \(+1.08\ \text{V}\) results from a sign error, adding the correction instead of subtracting it: \(0.93 + (0.0296)(5) = 1.08\ \text{V}\). This is equivalent to inverting \(Q\) and increases the potential, which is the wrong direction since \(Q > 1\) must lower \(E_{\text{cell}}\). (Choice B) \(+0.90\ \text{V}\) results from treating \(\log Q\) as 1 instead of 5, i.e., omitting the square on \([\text{Ag}^{+}]\) and computing \(0.93 - (0.0296)(1) \approx 0.90\ \text{V}\); this ignores that the silver term is squared in \(Q\). (Choice D) \(+1.22\ \text{V}\) results from using \(n = 1\) (so \(\frac{0.0592}{1} = 0.0592\)) together with a sign error: \(0.93 + (0.0592)(5) \approx 1.22\ \text{V}\). Both the electron count and the direction of the correction are wrong. Skill 2$ex75$,
    'hard', '4C', $cc75$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc75$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
