UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a75B$Forgets to square the silver term, taking log Q = 1 instead of 5$a75B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'scale_unit_error', $a75D$Uses n = 1 in 0.0592/n and also reverses the correction sign$a75D$ FROM q;

-- Q8 [Nernst Equation & Concentration Cells · hard · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans D] (g76)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Nernst Equation & Concentration Cells',
    $qt76$A galvanic concentration cell is built with two silver electrodes, each dipped in a solution of \(\text{AgNO}_3\). One half-cell contains \(0.0010\ \text{M}\ \text{Ag}^+\) and the other contains \(0.10\ \text{M}\ \text{Ag}^+\); the half-cells are joined by a salt bridge and the electrodes by an external wire. The relevant half-reaction is \(\text{Ag}^{+} + e^{-} \rightarrow \text{Ag}\) with \(E^{\circ} = +0.80\ \text{V}\), and at 298 K the Nernst equation may be used as \(E_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} - \frac{0.0592}{n}\log Q\). At the instant the cell is connected, which value of \(E_{\text{cell}}\) and which direction of electron flow through the external wire are both correct?$qt76$,
    $op76$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(E_{\\text{cell}} = +0.80\\ \\text{V}\\); electrons flow through the wire from the \\(0.10\\ \\text{M}\\) electrode to the \\(0.0010\\ \\text{M}\\) electrode."},{"label":"B","text":"\\(E_{\\text{cell}} = +0.118\\ \\text{V}\\); electrons flow through the wire from the \\(0.10\\ \\text{M}\\) electrode to the \\(0.0010\\ \\text{M}\\) electrode."},{"label":"C","text":"\\(E_{\\text{cell}} = +0.059\\ \\text{V}\\); electrons flow through the wire from the \\(0.0010\\ \\text{M}\\) electrode to the \\(0.10\\ \\text{M}\\) electrode."},{"label":"D","text":"\\(E_{\\text{cell}} = +0.118\\ \\text{V}\\); electrons flow through the wire from the \\(0.0010\\ \\text{M}\\) electrode to the \\(0.10\\ \\text{M}\\) electrode."}]$op76$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex76$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because in a concentration cell both electrodes are identical, so \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{red,cathode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,anode}} = (+0.80) - (+0.80) = 0\ \text{V}\); the only driving force is the difference in \([\text{Ag}^{+}]\), and the cell spontaneously acts to equalize the two concentrations. To approach equilibrium, \(\text{Ag}^{+}\) must be consumed in the concentrated (\(0.10\ \text{M}\)) half-cell, so reduction \((\text{Ag}^{+} + e^{-} \rightarrow \text{Ag})\) occurs there, making the \(0.10\ \text{M}\) electrode the cathode; oxidation \((\text{Ag} \rightarrow \text{Ag}^{+} + e^{-})\) occurs in the dilute (\(0.0010\ \text{M}\)) half-cell, making it the anode. Electrons always travel from anode to cathode through the external wire, i.e., from the \(0.0010\ \text{M}\) electrode to the \(0.10\ \text{M}\) electrode. Writing \(Q\) for the spontaneous (dilute-as-anode) cell gives \(Q = \frac{[\text{Ag}^{+}]_{\text{anode}}}{[\text{Ag}^{+}]_{\text{cathode}}} = \frac{0.0010}{0.10} = 0.010\), so \(E_{\text{cell}} = 0 - \frac{0.0592}{1}\log(0.010) = -0.0592(-2) = +0.118\ \text{V}\), confirming a spontaneous galvanic cell (\(E_{\text{cell}} > 0\), \(\Delta G < 0\)). Both the magnitude and the electron-flow direction in D are therefore correct. (Choice A) This reports \(E^{\circ}\) of the silver half-reaction (\(+0.80\ \text{V}\)) as the cell potential, ignoring that in a concentration cell the identical electrodes cancel to give \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = 0\) and that the entire potential arises from the concentration term; the electron-flow direction is also reversed. (Choice B) The voltage magnitude \(+0.118\ \text{V}\) is computed correctly, but the electron-flow direction is reversed; sending electrons from the \(0.10\ \text{M}\) to the \(0.0010\ \text{M}\) electrode would build up \(\text{Ag}^{+}\) in the already-concentrated half-cell and drive the system away from equilibrium, which is non-spontaneous. (Choice C) This uses the correct electron-flow direction but mishandles the Nernst term, treating the 100-fold ratio as a single factor of ten \((\log 10 = 1)\) so that \(\frac{0.0592}{1}(1) = 0.059\ \text{V}\); the true ratio is \(0.10/0.0010 = 100\), giving \(\log 100 = 2\) and \(0.118\ \text{V}\). Skill 2$ex76$,
    'hard', '4C', $cc76$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc76$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a76A$uses the half-reaction E° as E°cell instead of recognizing identical electrodes cancel to zero$a76A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a76B$correct magnitude but electron flow reversed (concentrated to dilute)$a76B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a76C$miscounts the concentration ratio as 10-fold, halving the Nernst term$a76C$ FROM q;

-- Q9 [Nernst Equation & Concentration Cells · hard · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans A] (g77)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Nernst Equation & Concentration Cells',
    $qt77$Consider the galvanic cell \(\text{Cd(s)} \mid \text{Cd}^{2+}(0.10\ \text{M}) \parallel \text{Ag}^{+}(0.10\ \text{M}) \mid \text{Ag(s)}\), whose cell reaction is \(\text{Cd(s)} + 2\,\text{Ag}^{+} \rightarrow \text{Cd}^{2+} + 2\,\text{Ag(s)}\) with \(n = 2\). At 298 K the Nernst equation is \(E_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} - \frac{0.0592}{2}\log Q\). Starting from the conditions above, four independent modifications are proposed, each a single tenfold concentration change: (A) lower \([\text{Cd}^{2+}]\) to \(0.010\ \text{M}\); (B) raise \([\text{Ag}^{+}]\) to \(1.0\ \text{M}\); (C) raise \([\text{Cd}^{2+}]\) to \(1.0\ \text{M}\); (D) lower \([\text{Ag}^{+}]\) to \(0.010\ \text{M}\). Which single change raises \(E_{\text{cell}}\) by the greatest amount?$qt77$,
    $op77$[{"label":"A","text":"Raising \\([\\text{Ag}^{+}]\\) to \\(1.0\\ \\text{M}\\), which increases \\(E_{\\text{cell}}\\) by \\(+0.0592\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"Lowering \\([\\text{Cd}^{2+}]\\) to \\(0.010\\ \\text{M}\\), which increases \\(E_{\\text{cell}}\\) by \\(+0.0296\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"Raising \\([\\text{Cd}^{2+}]\\) to \\(1.0\\ \\text{M}\\), which increases \\(E_{\\text{cell}}\\) by \\(+0.0296\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"Lowering \\([\\text{Ag}^{+}]\\) to \\(0.010\\ \\text{M}\\), which increases \\(E_{\\text{cell}}\\) by \\(+0.0592\\ \\text{V}\\)"}]$op77$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex77$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The reaction quotient for \(\text{Cd(s)} + 2\,\text{Ag}^{+} \rightarrow \text{Cd}^{2+} + 2\,\text{Ag(s)}\) is \(Q = \frac{[\text{Cd}^{2+}]}{[\text{Ag}^{+}]^{2}}\); solids do not appear. Because \(E_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} - \frac{0.0592}{2}\log Q = E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} - 0.0296\log Q\), the cell potential rises only when a change makes \(Q\) (hence \(\log Q\)) smaller, and the size of the rise is \(0.0296\) V per unit decrease in \(\log Q\). The answer to this question is A because \([\text{Ag}^{+}]\) is squared in \(Q\): raising it tenfold (from 0.10 to 1.0 M) changes \([\text{Ag}^{+}]^{2}\) by a factor of \(100\), so \(\log Q\) drops by 2 units, giving \(\Delta E = -0.0296 \times (-2) = +0.0592\ \text{V}\). Quantitatively, baseline \(Q = \frac{0.10}{(0.10)^{2}} = 10\) (\(\log Q = 1\)); after the change \(Q = \frac{0.10}{(1.0)^{2}} = 0.10\) (\(\log Q = -1\)), so \(\Delta(\log Q) = -2\) and \(E_{\text{cell}}\) increases by \(0.0592\) V — the largest of the four, because the squared term gives any change to \([\text{Ag}^{+}]\) twice the leverage of an equal-magnitude change to the singly-appearing \([\text{Cd}^{2+}]\). (Choice B) Lowering \([\text{Cd}^{2+}]\) tenfold does decrease \(Q\) and raise \(E_{\text{cell}}\), but \([\text{Cd}^{2+}]\) appears to the first power, so \(\log Q\) falls by only 1 unit and \(E_{\text{cell}}\) rises by just \(+0.0296\ \text{V}\); this is a real increase but only half as large as choice A. (Choice C) Raising \([\text{Cd}^{2+}]\) tenfold increases the numerator of \(Q\), so \(\log Q\) rises by 1 and \(E_{\text{cell}}\) decreases by \(0.0296\) V; treating this as an increase reverses the direction in which a numerator (product) term shifts the potential. (Choice D) Lowering \([\text{Ag}^{+}]\) tenfold raises \(Q\) (the squared denominator shrinks 100-fold), so \(\log Q\) rises by 2 and \(E_{\text{cell}}\) decreases by \(0.0592\) V; the \(0.0592\) magnitude is correct for a squared species but the sign is reversed, since lowering a reactant concentration lowers a galvanic cell's potential. Skill 2$ex77$,
    'hard', '4C', $cc77$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc77$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'partial_truth', $a77B$Correct direction but ignores the squared term, treating all tenfold changes as equal in magnitude (+0.0296 V instead of +0.0592 V)$a77B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'reversed_relationship', $a77C$Raises a product (numerator) concentration, which lowers Ecell, yet is labeled as an increase$a77C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a77D$Correct +0.0592 magnitude from the squared Ag+ term but wrong sign: lowering the reactant lowers, not raises, Ecell$a77D$ FROM q;

-- Q10 [Batteries · easy · Skill 1 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans B] (g78)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Batteries',
    $qt78$A nickel-cadmium (Ni-Cd) cell is a secondary cell, whereas a common alkaline cell is a primary cell that must be discarded once it dies. Which property of the Ni-Cd cell accounts for this difference?$qt78$,
    $op78$[{"label":"A","text":"It produces a higher cell voltage than the alkaline cell, so it stores more energy per discharge."},{"label":"B","text":"Its discharge (galvanic) reaction can be driven in reverse by an external power source, restoring the original reactants."},{"label":"C","text":"It operates without a salt bridge or porous separator, so its electrodes never become depleted."},{"label":"D","text":"Its anode and cathode are made of the same metal, so the electrodes cannot be consumed during discharge."}]$op78$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex78$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because the defining feature of a secondary (rechargeable) cell is that its spontaneous discharge reaction is chemically reversible: applying an external power source that pushes current backward (an electrolytic process, \(E_{\text{cell}}<0\), \(\Delta G>0\) supplied by the charger) regenerates the original reactants from the discharge products. A primary cell such as an alkaline cell uses a discharge reaction that is not practically reversible, so once the reactants are consumed it must be discarded. The reversibility of the discharge chemistry, not voltage or construction, is what makes the Ni-Cd cell rechargeable. (Choice A) Cell voltage and energy stored per discharge do not determine rechargeability; a primary cell can have an equal or higher voltage yet still be non-rechargeable, so this confuses an unrelated property with the defining one. (Choice C) Rechargeable cells still contain separators/electrolytes and their reactants are consumed during discharge; what matters is that discharge can be reversed, not that electrodes are never depleted, so this is factually incorrect. (Choice D) The electrodes of a Ni-Cd cell are different materials (a cadmium electrode and a nickel oxyhydroxide electrode) and they do undergo redox change during discharge; identical, non-consumed electrodes is not the basis of rechargeability. Skill 1$ex78$,
    'easy', '4C', $cc78$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc78$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'adjacent_fact', $a78A$conflates higher voltage/energy with rechargeability$a78A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a78C$believes rechargeable cells avoid electrode depletion via no separator$a78C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a78D$thinks identical non-consumed electrodes enable recharging$a78D$ FROM q;

-- Q11 [Batteries · easy · Skill 1 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans C] (g79)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Batteries',
    $qt79$A smartphone manufacturer is comparing two prototype lithium-ion batteries, A and B, that have exactly the same mass. Under identical testing conditions, battery B is found to store a larger total amount of electrical energy than battery A. Energy density is defined as the amount of energy a battery stores per unit mass. Based on this information, which battery has the greater energy density, and what does that imply for a phone of fixed battery mass?$qt79$,
    $op79$[{"label":"A","text":"Both batteries have the same energy density, because energy density depends only on the battery's voltage and not on how much energy it stores."},{"label":"B","text":"Battery A has the greater energy density, so for a fixed battery mass a phone using A can run longer between charges."},{"label":"C","text":"Battery B has the greater energy density, so for a fixed battery mass a phone using B can run longer between charges."},{"label":"D","text":"Battery B has the greater energy density, but this means a phone using B must be made heavier to hold the additional energy."}]$op79$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex79$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because energy density is the energy stored per unit mass, i.e. energy density \(= \dfrac{\text{energy stored}}{\text{mass}}\). The two batteries have equal mass, so the one storing more total energy has the larger numerator over the same denominator and therefore the larger ratio. Since battery B stores more total energy than battery A at equal mass, B has the greater energy density. A higher energy density means more energy is packed into each gram of battery, so for a phone with a fixed battery mass, battery B supplies more usable energy and the phone can run longer between charges. (Choice A) This is a misconception about what energy density measures; energy density is energy per unit mass and reflects how much total energy is stored relative to mass, not voltage alone, and at equal mass the battery storing more energy is necessarily more energy-dense. (Choice B) This reverses the relationship: it correctly recalls the definition of energy density but assigns it to the battery that stores less energy, so it identifies the wrong battery as superior. (Choice D) This correctly identifies B as more energy-dense but then misapplies the concept: higher energy density is precisely what lets the same (or even less) mass hold more energy, so achieving the higher energy is not contingent on making the battery heavier. Skill 1$ex79$,
    'easy', '4C', $cc79$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc79$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a79A$Confuses energy density with voltage and denies the energy-per-mass relationship$a79A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a79B$Assigns higher energy density to the lower-energy battery at equal mass$a79B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'partial_truth', $a79D$Correctly identifies the denser battery but wrongly claims higher density requires added mass$a79D$ FROM q;

-- Q12 [Batteries · medium · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans D] (g80)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Batteries',
    $qt80$A lead-acid battery in a car delivers about \(2\ \text{V}\) per cell. When the driver turns the key, the battery drives current through the starter motor to crank the engine. Once the engine is running, the alternator drives current back through the battery in the reverse direction to recharge it. Which statement correctly classifies the cell during these two operations and the sign of \(\Delta G\) for the cell reaction proceeding in each direction?$qt80$,
    $op80$[{"label":"A","text":"The battery is an electrolytic cell with \\(\\Delta G > 0\\) during both cranking and charging, because current flows through the cell in both operations."},{"label":"B","text":"While cranking the starter the battery is an electrolytic cell with \\(\\Delta G > 0\\); while being charged by the alternator it is a galvanic cell with \\(\\Delta G < 0\\) for the reaction being driven."},{"label":"C","text":"The battery is a galvanic cell with \\(\\Delta G < 0\\) during both cranking and charging, because the same lead-acid chemistry is present in both operations."},{"label":"D","text":"While cranking the starter the battery is a galvanic cell with \\(\\Delta G < 0\\); while being charged by the alternator it is an electrolytic cell with \\(\\Delta G > 0\\) for the reaction being driven."}]$op80$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex80$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because a rechargeable cell switches roles depending on the direction in which its reaction is being driven. While cranking the starter, the battery discharges: its chemical reaction proceeds spontaneously, converting stored chemical energy into electrical energy that it supplies to the external circuit. A cell that spontaneously generates electrical work is a galvanic (voltaic) cell, and spontaneity means the reaction has \(\Delta G < 0\) (equivalently \(E_{\text{cell}} > 0\)). During recharging, the alternator forces current backward through the cell, driving the reaction in its reverse, non-spontaneous direction to rebuild the reactants. A cell in which an external power source drives a non-spontaneous reaction is an electrolytic cell, and the reaction being driven has \(\Delta G > 0\) (its forward, spontaneous direction would have \(\Delta G < 0\), so the reverse direction the alternator forces has \(\Delta G > 0\)). Thus the same hardware behaves galvanically on discharge and electrolytically on charge, with the sign of \(\Delta G\) flipping because the reaction direction is reversed. (Choice A) Mere flow of current does not make a cell electrolytic; current also flows when a galvanic cell discharges. During cranking the cell discharges spontaneously and is galvanic with \(\Delta G < 0\), so labeling both operations electrolytic with \(\Delta G > 0\) is incorrect. (Choice B) This reverses the two operations: discharging to crank the starter is the spontaneous, galvanic mode, not the electrolytic one, and charging by the alternator is the externally driven, electrolytic mode, not the galvanic one. (Choice C) The chemistry being lead-acid in both operations does not keep the cell galvanic; classification depends on whether the reaction proceeds spontaneously (galvanic, discharge) or is forced by an external source (electrolytic, charge), so the charging step cannot have \(\Delta G < 0\) for the reaction being driven. Skill 2$ex80$,
    'medium', '4C', $cc80$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc80$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'partial_truth', $a80A$correctly notes current flows in both modes but wrongly concludes both are electrolytic with positive dG$a80A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a80B$swaps which operation is galvanic vs. electrolytic, calling discharge electrolytic and charge galvanic$a80B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a80C$assumes identical chemistry forces identical galvanic classification in both directions, missing the spontaneity reversal on charging$a80C$ FROM q;

-- Q13 [Batteries · medium · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans A] (g81)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Batteries',
    $qt81$A common disposable flashlight battery uses a zinc-based redox reaction that releases electrical energy spontaneously as the cell is used. Once the reactants are consumed, the cell is discarded because its internal reaction cannot be practically driven in reverse by applying an external voltage. Which of the following correctly classifies this battery and describes how it operates?$qt81$,
    $op81$[{"label":"A","text":"It is a primary cell that functions as a galvanic cell during use, converting chemical energy into electrical energy spontaneously, and it cannot be restored by an electrolytic recharging process."},{"label":"B","text":"It is a secondary cell that functions as a galvanic cell during use and can later be recharged by operating as an electrolytic cell."},{"label":"C","text":"It is a primary cell that functions as an electrolytic cell during use, consuming electrical energy to drive a nonspontaneous reaction."},{"label":"D","text":"It is a primary cell, but during normal use it operates as a galvanic cell only because an external power source continuously supplies the energy that drives its reaction."}]$op81$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex81$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because the defining feature of a primary cell is that it is disposable: its internal redox reaction is not practically reversible, so once the reactants are spent the cell is discarded rather than recharged. The stem states exactly this (it "cannot be practically driven in reverse"), which rules out a secondary (rechargeable) cell. While the cell is being used, it discharges spontaneously (\(\Delta G < 0\), \(E_{\text{cell}} > 0\)), which is by definition galvanic (voltaic) operation: chemical energy is converted into electrical energy with no external power source required. Because a primary cell's reaction cannot be reversed by applying an external voltage, it cannot be returned to its charged state through an electrolytic (charging) process. Choice A captures all three points correctly: primary classification, galvanic discharge, and no electrolytic recharge. (Choice B) A secondary cell is rechargeable and does discharge galvanically and recharge electrolytically, but the stem explicitly states this cell's reaction cannot be reversed, so it is a primary cell, not a secondary cell. (Choice C) Discharging a battery to power a device is a spontaneous, energy-releasing process and is therefore galvanic, not electrolytic; electrolytic operation requires an external power source to drive a nonspontaneous reaction, which is the opposite of what a battery does while in use. (Choice D) A galvanic cell discharges spontaneously and needs no external power source; claiming that an external source continuously drives the reaction during normal use contradicts the definition of galvanic operation and confuses discharge with charging. Skill 2$ex81$,
    'medium', '4C', $cc81$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc81$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a81B$Confuses primary (disposable) with secondary (rechargeable) cell despite stated irreversibility$a81B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'reversed_relationship', $a81C$Labels spontaneous discharge as electrolytic, reversing galvanic vs. electrolytic operation$a81C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'partial_truth', $a81D$Correct primary/galvanic labels but wrongly requires an external power source during discharge$a81D$ FROM q;

-- Q14 [Batteries · hard · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans B] (g82)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Batteries',
    $qt82$A lead-acid car battery uses a \(\text{Pb}\) electrode and a \(\text{PbO}_2\) electrode immersed in aqueous \(\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4\). The relevant standard reduction potentials are \(\text{PbO}_2 + 4\text{H}^+ + \text{SO}_4^{2-} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{PbSO}_4 + 2\text{H}_2\text{O}\), \(E^{\circ} = +1.69\ \text{V}\), and \(\text{PbSO}_4 + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Pb} + \text{SO}_4^{2-}\), \(E^{\circ} = -0.36\ \text{V}\). During discharge the battery powers the car spontaneously; during recharge the alternator forces the reverse reactions. Tracking the \(\text{PbO}_2\)-based electrode (the electrode that is reduced as the battery discharges) through one complete discharge-then-recharge cycle, which statement correctly describes its electrode role, its half-reaction type, and its terminal sign in each mode?$qt82$,
    $op82$[{"label":"A","text":"On discharge it is the cathode (reduction) and on recharge it becomes the anode (oxidation); it is the positive terminal during discharge but the negative terminal during recharge."},{"label":"B","text":"On discharge it is the cathode (reduction) and on recharge it becomes the anode (oxidation); it is the positive terminal in both modes."},{"label":"C","text":"On discharge it is the cathode (reduction) and on recharge it remains the cathode (reduction); it is the positive terminal in both modes."},{"label":"D","text":"On discharge it is the anode (oxidation) and on recharge it becomes the cathode (reduction); it is the negative terminal in both modes."}]$op82$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex82$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because the electrode's role and half-reaction reverse between the two modes while its terminal sign does not. During discharge the battery is a galvanic (voltaic) cell: \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{red,cathode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,anode}} = (+1.69\ \text{V}) - (-0.36\ \text{V}) = +2.05\ \text{V} > 0\), so the process is spontaneous (\(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} < 0\)). The \(\text{PbO}_2\) electrode is where \(\text{PbO}_2\) is reduced to \(\text{PbSO}_4\) (Pb goes from \(+4\) to \(+2\)); reduction defines the cathode, so on discharge this electrode is the cathode, and in a galvanic cell the cathode is the positive terminal. On recharge the alternator drives the cell as an electrolytic cell (\(E_{\text{cell}} < 0\), \(\Delta G > 0\), non-spontaneous), forcing the reverse reaction: \(\text{PbSO}_4\) is oxidized back to \(\text{PbO}_2\) (Pb goes from \(+2\) to \(+4\)). Because oxidation now occurs at this electrode, it has become the anode. The counterintuitive part is the sign: in an electrolytic cell the anode is the positive terminal, so the electrode that was the positive cathode on discharge is now the positive anode on recharge. Its role flips (cathode \(\rightarrow\) anode) and its half-reaction flips (reduction \(\rightarrow\) oxidation), but its terminal sign stays positive throughout the cycle. (Choice A) This choice gets the role reversal and half-reaction reversal correct but flips the terminal sign to negative on recharge. That error comes from applying the galvanic-only rule "the anode is the negative terminal" to the recharge step. In an electrolytic cell the anode is positive, so this electrode remains the positive terminal during recharge, not negative. (Choice C) This choice correctly describes discharge (cathode, reduction, positive) but assumes the electrode keeps the same role on recharge. In a rechargeable cell the recharge step deliberately reverses every half-reaction; the electrode that was reduced on discharge must be oxidized on recharge, converting it from a cathode into an anode. Treating its role as unchanged captures only half of the cycle. (Choice D) This choice reverses the stem's premise by labeling the \(\text{PbO}_2\) electrode as the site of oxidation on discharge. The stem specifies this is the electrode that is reduced as the battery discharges, which makes it the cathode (not the anode) on discharge; oxidation on discharge occurs at the \(\text{Pb}\) electrode instead. Calling it negative in both modes compounds the error by inverting the galvanic cathode sign as well. Skill 2$ex82$,
    'hard', '4C', $cc82$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc82$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a82A$Applies the galvanic-only rule 'anode is negative' to the electrolytic recharge mode, where the anode is actually positive$a82A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a82C$Correct for discharge but assumes the electrode keeps its cathode role on recharge, forgetting the half-reaction reverses$a82C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a82D$Inverts the stem's premise by making the reduced-on-discharge electrode the anode/oxidation site and negative in both modes$a82D$ FROM q;

-- Q15 [Isoelectric Focusing · easy · Skill 2 · 5C Separation and Purification Methods (BC) · ans C] (g83)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Isoelectric Focusing',
    $qt83$In isoelectric focusing (IEF), a stable pH gradient is set up across a gel — acidic near the anode and increasingly basic toward the cathode — and a voltage is applied (the cathode is the negative electrode, the anode is the positive electrode). Unlike ordinary electrophoresis, where a charged species migrates continuously toward one electrode, in IEF each amino acid migrates only until it reaches the position where the local pH equals its isoelectric point (pI), at which point it stops and concentrates. Glycine (pI = 6.0) is deposited at a spot in the gel where the local pH is 3.0. Toward which electrode does glycine migrate, and what causes it to ultimately stop?$qt83$,
    $op83$[{"label":"A","text":"Toward the cathode, continuing all the way to the cathode because the applied field drives it there regardless of the local pH"},{"label":"B","text":"Toward the anode, moving into progressively lower-pH regions until the local pH reaches 6.0, where its net charge becomes zero and it halts"},{"label":"C","text":"Toward the cathode, moving into progressively higher-pH regions until the local pH reaches 6.0, where its net charge becomes zero and it halts"},{"label":"D","text":"Toward the anode, because the acidic region near the anode matches glycine's environment and stabilizes its neutral form there"}]$op83$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex83$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Separation and Purification Methods (BC)'. The answer to this question is C because the defining feature of IEF is that the pH gradient makes migration self-limiting: at the starting position the local pH (3.0) is below glycine's pI (6.0), so the molecule is net positively charged and is pulled toward the negative electrode, the cathode. Because the gradient is acidic at the anode and basic toward the cathode, moving toward the cathode carries glycine into progressively higher-pH regions. As the local pH rises and approaches 6.0, glycine is increasingly deprotonated and its net charge shrinks toward zero; exactly at the position where local pH = pI = 6.0 the net charge is zero, there is no electrophoretic force, and the molecule stops and focuses. The endpoint is therefore set by the gradient (the pH = pI position), not by the electrode itself. (Choice A) This choice describes ordinary electrophoresis, in which a charged species travels continuously to the electrode; it ignores the IEF mechanism, in which the molecule loses its charge and halts at the pH = pI position before reaching the electrode. (Choice B) This choice reverses the gradient: the anode side is acidic, so migrating toward the anode would take glycine into progressively lower pH (further from 6.0), making it more positive rather than neutral, so it could never reach a pH = pI stopping point in that direction. (Choice D) This choice both sends the cation to the wrong (positive) electrode and invokes a nonexistent stabilization rule; glycine is neutral only where local pH equals its pI of 6.0, not in the acidic region near the anode where pH 3.0 leaves it net positive. Skill 2$ex83$,
    'easy', '5C', $cc83$Separation and Purification Methods (BC)$cc83$, 'biochemistry', 2, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a83A$Treats IEF like plain electrophoresis: species runs all the way to the electrode rather than focusing at pH = pI$a83A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a83B$Right focusing logic but reverses the acidic-anode/basic-cathode gradient direction$a83B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a83D$Sends cation to the wrong electrode and invents an acidic-region stabilization for the neutral form$a83D$ FROM q;

-- Q16 [Isoelectric Focusing · medium · Skill 2 · 5C Separation and Purification Methods (BC) · ans D] (g84)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Isoelectric Focusing',
    $qt84$Aspartate (\(\text{pI} = 2.8\)) is loaded onto an immobilized pH gradient that runs from \(\text{pH}\ 2\) at the anode to \(\text{pH}\ 10\) at the cathode, and a constant electric field is applied across the strip. The aspartate migrates through the gradient and then comes to rest as a sharp band at the position where the local gradient pH is \(2.8\), where it remains as long as the field is on. Which of the following best explains why the aspartate stops migrating precisely at the \(\text{pH}\ 2.8\) band?$qt84$,
    $op84$[{"label":"A","text":"The gel matrix has its smallest pore size at the \\(\\text{pH}\\ 2.8\\) band, so aspartate is sieved and physically trapped there by its size."},{"label":"B","text":"At \\(\\text{pH}\\ 2.8\\) aspartate carries its maximum net charge, so the field grips it most tightly and anchors it at that point."},{"label":"C","text":"Aspartate is least soluble at \\(\\text{pH}\\ 2.8\\), so it precipitates out of solution and can no longer move through the field."},{"label":"D","text":"At \\(\\text{pH}\\ 2.8\\) the net charge on aspartate is zero, so the electric field exerts no net electrophoretic force (\\(F = qE = 0\\)) and migration halts."}]$op84$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex84$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Separation and Purification Methods (BC)'. The answer to this question is D because the electrophoretic force on a species is \(F = qE\), the product of its net charge \(q\) and the field strength \(E\). As aspartate migrates through the pH gradient, the local pH continuously changes its net charge: at a pH above its pI it is deprotonated and net negative (and moves toward the anode/lower pH), while at a pH below its pI it is protonated and net positive (and moves toward the cathode/higher pH). At the point where the gradient pH equals the pI of \(2.8\), the positive and negative charges on aspartate exactly cancel, giving \(q = 0\). With zero net charge, \(F = qE = 0\) regardless of the field strength, so the field can no longer drive the molecule, and it stops — it "focuses." The band is sharp and self-correcting: any drift toward higher pH makes the molecule net negative and pulls it back toward the anode, and any drift toward lower pH makes it net positive and pushes it back, so it returns to the \(\text{pH} = \text{pI}\) position. (Choice A) This imports the size-sieving mechanism of SDS-PAGE; isoelectric focusing separates by charge/pI within a pH gradient, and stopping is set by where net charge reaches zero, not by pore size or molecular size. (Choice B) This reverses the actual charge relationship: at its pI a molecule's net charge is at a minimum (zero), not a maximum; a larger charge would produce a larger force and faster migration, not anchoring. (Choice C) Although solubility is often lowest near the pI, isoelectric focusing stops the molecule because the driving force vanishes at zero net charge, not because of precipitation; the molecule remains in solution and would resume migrating if the local pH were changed. Skill 2$ex84$,
    'medium', '5C', $cc84$Separation and Purification Methods (BC)$cc84$, 'biochemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a84A$Imports SDS-PAGE size-sieving mechanism into a charge-based IEF separation$a84A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a84B$Claims maximum net charge at pI anchors the molecule, reversing the true zero-charge condition$a84B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a84C$Attributes stopping to pI precipitation/insolubility rather than loss of electrophoretic driving force$a84C$ FROM q;

-- Q17 [Isoelectric Focusing · medium · Skill 2 · 5C Separation and Purification Methods (BC) · ans A] (g85)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Isoelectric Focusing',
    $qt85$During isoelectric focusing (IEF), a researcher loads a mixture of amino acids onto a gel containing a stable, continuous pH gradient that runs from low pH at the anode (positive electrode) to high pH at the cathode (negative electrode), then applies the field. One species is free arginine, a "basic" amino acid with \(\text{pI} = 10.8\). At the instant the field is switched on, this arginine is sitting in a region buffered to \(\text{pH} = 12.0\). Assuming the run continues until the system reaches its steady state, where in the gradient will the arginine ultimately come to rest, and what holds it there?$qt85$,
    $op85$[{"label":"A","text":"It moves toward the anode until it reaches the \\(\\text{pH} = 10.8\\) band, where its net charge becomes zero so the field no longer drives it; any drift away from that band re-ionizes it and pushes it back, holding it in place"},{"label":"B","text":"It moves toward the cathode until it reaches the \\(\\text{pH} = 10.8\\) band, where its net charge becomes zero; any drift away re-ionizes it and pushes it back, holding it in place"},{"label":"C","text":"It continues toward the anode all the way to the low-pH end of the gel, because once a molecule starts migrating in an applied field it keeps moving until it reaches the electrode"},{"label":"D","text":"It stops immediately at \\(\\text{pH} = 12.0\\), because the high local pH neutralizes the basic side chain and a neutral molecule cannot migrate in any field"}]$op85$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex85$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Separation and Purification Methods (BC)'. The answer to this question is A because IEF separates molecules by the pH at which their net charge is zero, exploiting the fact that net charge sign is set by local pH relative to pI. At the start, the local \(\text{pH} = 12.0 > \text{pI} = 10.8\), so the arginine is deprotonated past its zwitterionic point and carries a NET NEGATIVE charge. A net-negative species is attracted to the positive electrode (the anode), which here is at the LOW-pH end of the gradient, so the molecule migrates toward the anode and into progressively lower-pH regions. The key IEF dynamic is what happens as it travels: the moment it crosses into pH below 10.8 it would become net POSITIVE and be pushed back toward the cathode (toward higher pH). It therefore cannot pass the \(\text{pH} = 10.8\) band — at exactly \(\text{pH} = \text{pI}\) the net charge is zero, the electric force vanishes, and the molecule stops. This makes the pI band a self-correcting steady-state trap: diffusion away from it re-ionizes the molecule with the sign needed to drive it back, which is precisely why IEF produces sharp, focused bands rather than a smear. (Choice B) This correctly describes the focusing-trap mechanism but reverses the migration direction: a net-negative species (pH > pI) is pulled to the anode, not the cathode, and in this gradient the anode is at low pH, so the molecule moves toward lower pH (the anode) to reach its pI, not toward the cathode. (Choice C) This treats migration as ballistic — once moving, always moving to the electrode — which is the behavior of ordinary fixed-pH electrophoresis, not IEF; it ignores that the changing local pH flips the charge sign at the pI and arrests the molecule before it reaches the electrode. (Choice D) This confuses the static charge-sign rule with the IEF endpoint: at \(\text{pH} = 12.0\) (above the pI) the molecule is net negative, not neutral, so it does migrate; neutrality and arrest occur only when it reaches \(\text{pH} = \text{pI} = 10.8\), not at the starting position. Skill 2$ex85$,
    'medium', '5C', $cc85$Separation and Purification Methods (BC)$cc85$, 'biochemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a85B$correct focusing-trap mechanism but inverts migration direction (sends net-negative species to cathode/high-pH instead of anode/low-pH to reach pI)$a85B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a85C$ballistic-migration misconception: treats IEF like fixed-pH electrophoresis where a moving ion always reaches the electrode, ignoring charge-sign reversal at pI$a85C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a85D$applies the at-pI neutral/stationary endpoint to the starting pH=12.0 position, mistaking 'above pI' for 'neutral'$a85D$ FROM q;

-- Q18 [Isoelectric Focusing · hard · Skill 2 · 5C Separation and Purification Methods (BC) · ans B] (g86)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Isoelectric Focusing',
    $qt86$In a fixed-pH electrophoresis chamber, a buffer holds the entire separation field at a uniform pH of 9.0 (this is not a pH gradient). A mixture containing aspartate (pI 2.8), glycine (pI 6.0), and lysine (pI 9.7) is loaded at the center, and a voltage is applied so that the cathode lies on the left and the anode lies on the right. A residue is net negative when the buffer pH lies above its pI and net positive when the pH lies below its pI, and its migration speed grows as the difference between the buffer pH and its pI increases. Which of the following correctly describes the migration of all three residues?$qt86$,
    $op86$[{"label":"A","text":"Aspartate and glycine migrate toward the cathode (aspartate faster than glycine), while lysine migrates toward the anode."},{"label":"B","text":"Aspartate and glycine migrate toward the anode (aspartate faster than glycine), while lysine migrates toward the cathode."},{"label":"C","text":"Aspartate and glycine migrate toward the anode (glycine faster than aspartate), while lysine migrates toward the cathode."},{"label":"D","text":"Aspartate and glycine migrate toward the anode, while lysine also migrates toward the anode."}]$op86$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex86$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Separation and Purification Methods (BC)'. The answer to this question is B because at the fixed buffer pH of 9.0 each residue's charge is fixed by comparing the pH to its pI: a residue is net negative when pH > pI (and migrates toward the positive anode) and net positive when pH < pI (and migrates toward the negative cathode). For aspartate, \(9.0 > 2.8\), so it is net negative and moves toward the anode; for glycine, \(9.0 > 6.0\), so it is also net negative and moves toward the anode; for lysine, \(9.0 < 9.7\), so it is net positive and moves toward the cathode. Migration speed scales with \(|\text{pH} - \text{pI}|\): aspartate has \(|9.0 - 2.8| = 6.2\) while glycine has \(|9.0 - 6.0| = 3.0\), so aspartate carries the larger net negative charge and migrates faster than glycine. The correct picture is therefore aspartate and glycine both toward the anode with aspartate faster, and lysine toward the cathode. (Choice A) reverses the charge rule by sending the two net-negative residues (aspartate and glycine) toward the cathode and the net-positive residue (lysine) toward the anode; in fact anions move toward the anode and cations toward the cathode, so every electrode assignment here is inverted. (Choice C) gets the electrode directions right but swaps the speed order, claiming glycine migrates faster than aspartate; because \(|9.0 - 6.0| = 3.0\) is smaller than \(|9.0 - 2.8| = 6.2\), glycine actually carries the smaller charge and migrates more slowly than aspartate. (Choice D) correctly routes aspartate and glycine to the anode but sends lysine to the anode as well; lysine is net positive at pH 9.0 because \(9.0 < 9.7\), so it must migrate toward the cathode, not the anode. Skill 2$ex86$,
    'hard', '5C', $cc86$Separation and Purification Methods (BC)$cc86$, 'biochemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a86A$inverts the pH-versus-pI charge rule, sending net-negative residues to the cathode and the net-positive residue to the anode$a86A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a86C$correct electrode directions but swaps the |pH - pI| speed ordering of the two anode-bound residues$a86C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'partial_truth', $a86D$correct for aspartate and glycine but misassigns net-positive lysine to the anode instead of the cathode$a86D$ FROM q;

